title: claim expiry ignores UTC offsets in RFC3339 timestamps, and warns on every
  pick
updated: 2026-08-10T01:27:58Z
---

`lib/App/karr/Role/ClaimTimeout.pm:26`:

    Time::Piece->strptime($task->claimed_at =~ s/Z$//r, '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S')

Only a trailing `Z` is stripped. kanban-md writes RFC3339 with a real offset and
fractional seconds — verified against the actual binary:
`2026-08-09T17:28:46.449764553+02:00`. The offset and the fraction are silently
discarded, so the timestamp is read as if it were UTC.

    # claim genuinely 2.5 h old, stamped +02:00, timeout 1h
    karr pick --claim thief -> "No available tasks to pick."   # expired claim never expires
    # claim genuinely 10 min old, stamped -05:00, timeout 1h
    karr pick --claim thief -> "Picked task 1 ... (claimed by thief)"  # live claim stolen

On top of that, every call prints to STDERR:

    Garbage at end of string in strptime: .123456789+02:00 at Time/Piece.pm line 598

So a karr agent working next to a kanban-md agent either never releases stale
claims or robs live ones, and warns on every pick. Fix: parse full RFC3339
(offset + fractional seconds) and normalise to UTC before comparing.
Fixed and merged into main; full suite green at 88 files / 566 tests.

Every one of these was reproduced before it was fixed and left a regression test
that was verified to fail against the pre-fix code -- not a test that merely
passes now. Details and what was deliberately NOT fixed are in the merge commit
that names this ticket.
